<p>President Trump said direct U.S.-Iran talks could resume within days, likely again in Islamabad, even as the U.S. military says it has fully imposed a blockade on maritime trade tied to Iranian ports. At the same time, multiple reports say mediators are trying to extend the current two-week ceasefire, which officials said is due to expire on April 22, to buy more time for diplomacy. The main sticking point has not changed. Washington wants Iran to make an affirmative commitment not to seek a nuclear weapon, while Tehran has rejected what it calls unrealistic U.S. demands and tied progress to issues including sanctions relief and control over access to the Strait of Hormuz. Meanwhile, the blockade has raised tensions around one of the world’s most important shipping corridors, and Iran has warned it could retaliate by disrupting broader regional trade routes if the pressure continues. Still, there are a few reasons for cautious optimism. Several outlets say back-channel contacts have narrowed some gaps, oil prices fell for a second straight day on hopes for renewed talks, and Pakistan has kept up an active mediation effort that both sides appear willing to use if negotiations restart this week.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/cZhuuDIjIuGy3WQ9aGsYkrr_AW2wlYhHLkHmi8CL66Otvc-zO4ZYJ98meo10wj5-3vxOEcTF0PjdNR3VCnw2o-NCVaWfkZRuWxNfbwtnz-ffug6rZx56saCgIsjC1CvEBMzyya3JOUjNK4ra9CjVgLLF3LZNtqSW0uQ9xUJxFse3GVa3Gu52s9K2i73UAtV7O_EzpFDO3EF5gA' alt='President Trump at the White House on Monday.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Trump will not attend: He said a new round of talks could happen this week but ruled out participating personally, leaving open who would represent Washington if negotiators meet again.</li><li>Shipping evidence: Despite U.S. claims that Iranian maritime trade has been halted, shipping data and other reports cited several vessels, including at least one sanctioned tanker, still moving through Hormuz or trying to do so.</li><li>Chinese exposure: A fully laden Chinese tanker turned back twice in 48 hours, showing how the blockade is shaping commercial decisions far beyond Iran and the United States.</li><li>Domestic pressure: Analysts told the South China Morning Post that Trump’s focus on ending the war reflects growing pressure from high oil and gasoline prices and from a conflict that has lasted longer than promised.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>United States: Vice President JD Vance said the talks failed because Iran would not make an affirmative commitment that it would neither seek a nuclear weapon nor the tools needed to obtain one quickly. (<a href='https://www.politico.com/news/2026/04/15/trump-iran-war-peace-negotiations-00872682'>Politico</a>)</li><li>Iran: Tehran says the Islamabad negotiations broke down over U.S. demands it sees as unrealistic, and it has warned that naval pressure could jeopardize the ceasefire and trigger broader trade retaliation. (<a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/4/15/iran-warns-us-naval-blockade-threatens-ceasefire'>Al Jazeera</a>)</li><li>Pakistan: Pakistan’s military leadership has kept up its mediation effort, with army chief Asim Munir working to bring both sides back to Islamabad for another round. (<a href='https://www.ft.com/content/40218940-4f0f-4aee-9209-2fb713eea736'>Financial Times</a>)</li><li>Analysts: Some analysts say the blockade is a high-stakes tactic with real practical limits, because enforcing a trade cutoff around Hormuz is difficult and risks wider market disruption. (<a href='https://english.elpais.com/international/2026-04-15/donald-trumps-double-blockade-of-hormuz-a-high-stakes-gamble-with-many-limitations.html'>El País</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.rferl.org/a/trump-peace-talks-iran-blockade-china-russia/33732708.html'>Trump Says Peace Talks Could Resume Soon As Blockade Continues</a> - rferl.org</li><li><a href='https://www.npr.org/2026/04/15/nx-s1-5786034/iran-middle-east-updates'>Trump says I